topcomIsRegularTriangulation -- determine if a given triangulation is a regular triangulation
*********************************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        topcomIsRegularTriangulation(A, tri)
  * Inputs:
      * A, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, A $d \times n$ matrix over
        ZZ.  Each column represents one of the points which can be used in a
        triangulation
      * tri, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, A triangulation of the point
        set C
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * Homogenize => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default
        value true, If true, $A$ determines a point configuration, and the
        matrix is then "homogenized": a row of 1's is appended to $A$, creating
        a vector configuration of $n$ vectors in $\mathbb{R}^{d+1}$.
  * Outputs:
      * a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, whether the given
        triangulation is regular

Description
===========

The following example is one of the simplest examples of a non-regular
triangulation.  Notice that tri is a triangulation of the polytope which is the
convex hull of the columns of $A$, which are the only points allowed in the
triangulation.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : A = transpose matrix {{0,3},{0,1},{-1,-1},{1,-1},{-4,-2},{4,-2}}        |
|                                                                             |
|o1 = | 0 0 -1 1  -4 4  |                                                     |
|     | 3 1 -1 -1 -2 -2 |                                                     |
|                                                                             |
|              2       6                                                      |
|o1 : Matrix ZZ  <-- ZZ                                                       |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : tri = {{0,1,2}, {1,3,5}, {2,3,4}, {0,1,5},                              |
|         {0,2,4}, {3,4,5}, {1,2,3}}                                          |
|                                                                             |
|o2 = {{0, 1, 2}, {1, 3, 5}, {2, 3, 4}, {0, 1, 5}, {0, 2, 4}, {3, 4, 5}, {1,  |
|     ------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|     2, 3}}                                                                  |
|                                                                             |
|o2 : List                                                                    |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : topcomIsRegularTriangulation(A,tri)                                     |
|                                                                             |
|o3 = false                                                                   |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : assert not topcomIsRegularTriangulation(A,tri)                          |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : assert topcomIsTriangulation(A, tri)                                    |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

Setting debugLevel to either 1,2, or 5 will give more detail about what files
are written to Topcom, and what the executable is. Setting debugLevel to 0
means that the function will run silently.

Caveat
======

Do we check that the triangulation is actually well defined?

See also
========

  * *note topcomRegularFineTriangulation:
    topcomRegularFineTriangulation_lpMatrix_rp, -- compute a regular
    triangulation using all of the given points
  * *note topcomIsTriangulation: topcomIsTriangulation_lpMatrix_cmList_rp, --
    determine if a set of subsets is a triangulation of a point set

Ways to use topcomIsRegularTriangulation:
=========================================

  * "topcomIsRegularTriangulation(Matrix,List)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note topcomIsRegularTriangulation: topcomIsRegularTriangulation, is
a *note method function with options: (Macaulay2Doc)MethodFunctionWithOptions,.

